1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,628
In November 1983, a U.S. advisory board

2
00:00:03,648 --> 00:00:06,676
later concluded, some Soviet forces may

3
00:00:06,696 --> 00:00:09,646
have been preparing for a NATO nuclear

4
00:00:09,666 --> 00:00:10,193
strike.

5
00:00:10,663 --> 00:00:11,653
It was only a drill.

6
00:00:12,123 --> 00:00:14,253
So why did some in Moscow fear it was

7
00:00:14,273 --> 00:00:14,543
real?

8
00:00:15,113 --> 00:00:17,453
This is the cover of a secret report

9
00:00:17,473 --> 00:00:19,682
written for the President in 1990.

10
00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:21,942
Its title: The Soviet War Scare.

11
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:25,157
It stayed classified for 25 years, until

12
00:00:25,177 --> 00:00:27,991
the National Security Archive, after what

13
00:00:28,011 --> 00:00:30,825
it calls a 12-year fight, got it released

14
00:00:30,845 --> 00:00:31,931
in part in 2015.

15
00:00:32,501 --> 00:00:34,694
Its authors, the President's Foreign

16
00:00:34,714 --> 00:00:37,030
Intelligence Advisory Board, looked at

17
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:39,611
hundreds of documents and interviewed more

18
00:00:39,631 --> 00:00:42,009
than 75 American and British officials.

19
00:00:42,479 --> 00:00:44,534
About the NATO exercise called Able

20
00:00:44,554 --> 00:00:45,661
Archer, they wrote:

21
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:48,739
There is little doubt in our minds that

22
00:00:48,759 --> 00:00:51,462
the Soviets were genuinely worried by Able

23
00:00:51,482 --> 00:00:54,184
Archer; however, the depth of that concern

24
00:00:54,204 --> 00:00:55,611
is difficult to gauge.

25
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:57,784
Today we read the paper trail:

26
00:00:58,254 --> 00:01:00,527
the after action report by the U.S.

27
00:01:00,547 --> 00:01:03,015
Strategic Air Command's observer team,

28
00:01:03,485 --> 00:01:05,732
the Soviet side as American intelligence

29
00:01:05,752 --> 00:01:06,128
saw it,

30
00:01:06,598 --> 00:01:08,527
the memo that reached President Reagan,

31
00:01:08,997 --> 00:01:11,624
and the argument inside U.S. intelligence

32
00:01:11,644 --> 00:01:13,689
over whether any of it was real.

33
00:01:14,259 --> 00:01:16,869
Everything here comes from declassified

34
00:01:16,889 --> 00:01:19,430
American records, a CIA study, and the

35
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:21,857
National Security Archive's summary.

36
00:01:22,327 --> 00:01:24,678
Where historians and officials disagree,

37
00:01:24,698 --> 00:01:25,270
we say so.

38
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:27,862
And we never claim more than the documents

39
00:01:27,882 --> 00:01:28,117
show.

40
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:38,515
Start with what Able Archer actually was.

41
00:01:39,135 --> 00:01:40,664
An annual NATO exercise.

42
00:01:41,134 --> 00:01:43,557
A command post exercise: mainly staff

43
00:01:43,577 --> 00:01:46,330
officers at headquarters, though the Board

44
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:48,905
says some U.S. forces also took part in

45
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:49,698
live drills.

46
00:01:50,168 --> 00:01:52,828
The after action report calls it practice

47
00:01:52,848 --> 00:01:55,377
in command and control, with particular

48
00:01:55,397 --> 00:01:57,403
emphasis on the transition from

49
00:01:57,423 --> 00:01:59,953
conventional operations to chemical and

50
00:01:59,973 --> 00:02:01,195
nuclear operations.

51
00:02:01,765 --> 00:02:04,666
In 1983 it ran from November seventh to

52
00:02:04,686 --> 00:02:06,014
November eleventh.

53
00:02:06,484 --> 00:02:08,772
Three days of what the report calls low

54
00:02:08,792 --> 00:02:10,429
spectrum, conventional play.

55
00:02:10,899 --> 00:02:13,037
Then two days of high spectrum play:

56
00:02:13,057 --> 00:02:13,756
nuclear war.

57
00:02:14,326 --> 00:02:16,549
The report says that in the exercise

58
00:02:16,569 --> 00:02:19,166
scenario, orange forces attacked along the

59
00:02:19,186 --> 00:02:21,720
entire German border, with air attacks on

60
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,275
blue airfields, and used chemical weapons

61
00:02:24,295 --> 00:02:25,770
throughout the exercise.

62
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,576
On the second day, the report says, the

63
00:02:28,596 --> 00:02:30,659
Strategic Air Command observers were

64
00:02:30,679 --> 00:02:33,090
directed to an alternate war headquarters.

65
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,919
And it notes that helmets, gas masks and

66
00:02:35,939 --> 00:02:37,644
chemical suits were required.

67
00:02:38,114 --> 00:02:40,448
Gas masks were worn for hours after a

68
00:02:40,468 --> 00:02:42,102
simulated chemical attack.

69
00:02:42,671 --> 00:02:45,302
The Strategic Air Command sent a small

70
00:02:45,322 --> 00:02:47,884
team to observe how B-52 bombers were

71
00:02:47,904 --> 00:02:48,232
used.

72
00:02:48,702 --> 00:02:50,838
Earlier, the report notes, SAC

73
00:02:50,858 --> 00:02:53,856
participation had been discouraged because

74
00:02:53,876 --> 00:02:56,227
of, quote, the possible political

75
00:02:56,247 --> 00:02:58,670
implications or inferences of B-52

76
00:02:58,690 --> 00:02:59,532
involvement.

77
00:03:00,103 --> 00:03:01,903
Then comes a line that reads very

78
00:03:01,923 --> 00:03:02,896
differently today.

79
00:03:03,316 --> 00:03:06,185
Quote: Numerous times during the exercise,

80
00:03:06,205 --> 00:03:08,937
the word strike was used in reference to

81
00:03:08,957 --> 00:03:09,832
B-52 sorties.

82
00:03:10,414 --> 00:03:12,635
While this is an obvious slip of the

83
00:03:12,655 --> 00:03:15,187
tongue and was quickly corrected, in most

84
00:03:15,207 --> 00:03:17,801
cases, it does serve to fuel any inference

85
00:03:17,821 --> 00:03:20,167
should a remark be made in a nonsecure

86
00:03:20,187 --> 00:03:20,914
environment.

87
00:03:21,484 --> 00:03:23,726
To be clear: the drill was routine, and

88
00:03:23,746 --> 00:03:25,931
nothing in these records suggests NATO

89
00:03:25,951 --> 00:03:26,976
planned to attack.

90
00:03:27,446 --> 00:03:29,565
The Advisory Board agrees it was a

91
00:03:29,585 --> 00:03:32,082
recurring event that Soviet intelligence

92
00:03:32,102 --> 00:03:33,215
normally monitors.

93
00:03:33,685 --> 00:03:36,394
But the 1983 version, the Board says, had

94
00:03:36,414 --> 00:03:37,859
some special wrinkles.

95
00:03:38,429 --> 00:03:40,483
New procedures for releasing nuclear

96
00:03:40,503 --> 00:03:40,945
weapons.

97
00:03:41,415 --> 00:03:43,757
Pre-exercise messages that notionally

98
00:03:43,777 --> 00:03:45,991
moved forces from normal readiness,

99
00:03:46,011 --> 00:03:48,672
through various alert phases, to a general

100
00:03:48,692 --> 00:03:49,056
alert.

101
00:03:49,526 --> 00:03:51,638
And the Board was told that some U.S.

102
00:03:51,658 --> 00:03:53,597
aircraft practiced nuclear warhead

103
00:03:53,617 --> 00:03:54,116
handling,

104
00:03:54,586 --> 00:03:56,267
taxiing out of hangars carrying

105
00:03:56,287 --> 00:03:58,078
realistic-looking dummy warheads.

106
00:03:58,647 --> 00:04:00,958
According to the National Security

107
00:04:00,978 --> 00:04:03,837
Archive, summarizing declassified NATO and

108
00:04:03,857 --> 00:04:06,579
Air Force documents, the exercise season

109
00:04:06,599 --> 00:04:09,115
also included a radio-silent airlift:

110
00:04:09,585 --> 00:04:13,205
170 flights carrying about 19,000 American

111
00:04:13,225 --> 00:04:14,852
soldiers to Europe.

112
00:04:15,423 --> 00:04:17,427
Now look at the map from the Soviet side.

113
00:04:18,046 --> 00:04:20,602
The Advisory Board says Soviet early

114
00:04:20,622 --> 00:04:23,392
warning radars could once give about 13

115
00:04:23,412 --> 00:04:26,039
minutes of warning of American ICBMs.

116
00:04:26,509 --> 00:04:29,038
New radars and satellites raised that to

117
00:04:29,058 --> 00:04:29,611
about 30.

118
00:04:30,081 --> 00:04:32,322
Then NATO began deploying Pershing II

119
00:04:32,342 --> 00:04:33,850
missiles in West Germany,

120
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:36,702
and by Soviet calculations, the Board

121
00:04:36,722 --> 00:04:39,364
says, warning of a first strike on Moscow

122
00:04:39,384 --> 00:04:41,636
fell, probably, to about 8 minutes.

123
00:04:42,206 --> 00:04:43,399
The Board adds a caution.

124
00:04:43,869 --> 00:04:46,243
The Pershing II's real range, 1,800

125
00:04:46,263 --> 00:04:49,047
kilometers, would not have reached Moscow

126
00:04:49,067 --> 00:04:50,278
from West Germany.

127
00:04:50,748 --> 00:04:53,462
Warsaw Pact sources, though, credited it

128
00:04:53,482 --> 00:04:54,213
with 2,500.

129
00:04:54,783 --> 00:04:55,731
And who decides?

130
00:04:56,201 --> 00:04:58,687
The Board says probably no more than three

131
00:04:58,707 --> 00:05:00,954
leaders could authorize Soviet nuclear

132
00:05:00,974 --> 00:05:01,431
weapons.

133
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:04,325
Under extreme time pressure, perhaps only

134
00:05:04,345 --> 00:05:06,531
the General Secretary and the Defense

135
00:05:06,551 --> 00:05:07,067
Minister.

136
00:05:07,638 --> 00:05:09,722
The man in the top job kept changing.

137
00:05:10,192 --> 00:05:12,316
The Board notes the post of General

138
00:05:12,336 --> 00:05:14,643
Secretary changed hands three times in

139
00:05:14,663 --> 00:05:15,379
three years.

140
00:05:15,849 --> 00:05:18,376
Brezhnev died in November 1982.

141
00:05:18,846 --> 00:05:21,121
Andropov, February 1984.

142
00:05:21,591 --> 00:05:23,712
Chernenko, March 1985.

143
00:05:24,282 --> 00:05:25,646
Andropov was gravely ill.

144
00:05:26,116 --> 00:05:28,124
The Board says kidney failure in late

145
00:05:28,144 --> 00:05:29,879
September led to a long illness.

146
00:05:30,349 --> 00:05:32,222
He failed to appear at the Kremlin

147
00:05:32,242 --> 00:05:34,060
celebrations on November seventh,

148
00:05:34,530 --> 00:05:35,984
the day Able Archer began.

149
00:05:36,554 --> 00:05:38,487
Behind the scare was an intelligence

150
00:05:38,507 --> 00:05:39,464
collection effort.

151
00:05:40,084 --> 00:05:42,866
In 1981, the KGB launched an operation

152
00:05:42,886 --> 00:05:45,741
called RYAN, from the Russian words for

153
00:05:45,761 --> 00:05:47,437
nuclear missile attack.

154
00:05:47,907 --> 00:05:50,207
Officers abroad were ordered to look for

155
00:05:50,227 --> 00:05:52,236
signs that the West was preparing a

156
00:05:52,256 --> 00:05:53,164
surprise strike.

157
00:05:53,734 --> 00:05:56,502
By 1984, the Board says, tasking from

158
00:05:56,522 --> 00:05:59,215
Moscow had grown curiously esoteric.

159
00:05:59,685 --> 00:06:01,617
London was told to watch the cruise

160
00:06:01,637 --> 00:06:03,401
missile base at Greenham Common,

161
00:06:03,871 --> 00:06:05,436
and to keep an eye on banks,

162
00:06:05,456 --> 00:06:07,304
slaughterhouses and post offices.

163
00:06:07,874 --> 00:06:10,536
The report gives one small example of the

164
00:06:10,556 --> 00:06:12,107
KGB's reporting demands.

165
00:06:12,577 --> 00:06:15,057
A local campaign for blood donors met a

166
00:06:15,077 --> 00:06:17,556
requirement to report evidence of blood

167
00:06:17,576 --> 00:06:18,005
drives.

168
00:06:18,475 --> 00:06:19,572
So it was reported.

169
00:06:20,042 --> 00:06:22,087
And headquarters praised the London

170
00:06:22,107 --> 00:06:24,448
office, citing the blood donor report as

171
00:06:24,468 --> 00:06:25,805
especially interesting.

172
00:06:26,375 --> 00:06:28,951
A self-reinforcing cycle, the Board called

173
00:06:28,971 --> 00:06:29,136
it:

174
00:06:29,719 --> 00:06:31,557
leadership concern provoked more

175
00:06:31,577 --> 00:06:32,138
reporting,

176
00:06:32,721 --> 00:06:34,683
and more reporting added fuel to

177
00:06:34,703 --> 00:06:35,983
leadership anxieties.

178
00:06:36,553 --> 00:06:38,803
According to the National Security

179
00:06:38,823 --> 00:06:41,206
Archive's summary of the report, the

180
00:06:41,226 --> 00:06:43,742
program reportedly included a computer

181
00:06:43,762 --> 00:06:46,145
model: a database of 40,000 weighted

182
00:06:46,165 --> 00:06:48,882
military, political and economic factors.

183
00:06:49,352 --> 00:06:51,803
Before long, the report says, it started

184
00:06:51,823 --> 00:06:53,532
spewing very unwelcome news.

185
00:06:54,102 --> 00:06:57,035
In January 1984, the head of the KGB's

186
00:06:57,055 --> 00:07:00,221
foreign intelligence directorate, General

187
00:07:00,241 --> 00:07:03,096
Kryuchkov, told his officers that the

188
00:07:03,116 --> 00:07:05,660
threat of nuclear war had reached

189
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,370
dangerous proportions.

190
00:07:07,952 --> 00:07:10,386
They must not, he said, fail to perceive

191
00:07:10,406 --> 00:07:12,902
direct preparation by the adversary for a

192
00:07:12,922 --> 00:07:14,313
nuclear missile attack.

193
00:07:14,883 --> 00:07:15,975
Now the year itself.

194
00:07:16,595 --> 00:07:17,847
March 1983.

195
00:07:18,317 --> 00:07:21,098
President Reagan calls the Soviet Union an

196
00:07:21,118 --> 00:07:21,899
evil empire.

197
00:07:22,369 --> 00:07:24,862
And two weeks later he announces a missile

198
00:07:24,882 --> 00:07:27,256
defense research program the press calls

199
00:07:27,276 --> 00:07:27,855
Star Wars.

200
00:07:28,425 --> 00:07:30,258
The Soviet media answered in kind.

201
00:07:30,728 --> 00:07:33,063
According to a CIA study, Reagan was

202
00:07:33,083 --> 00:07:35,811
repeatedly compared to Hitler, and accused

203
00:07:35,831 --> 00:07:37,708
of fanning the flames of war.

204
00:07:38,178 --> 00:07:40,818
General Secretary Andropov suggested the

205
00:07:40,838 --> 00:07:43,013
President was insane, and a liar.

206
00:07:43,583 --> 00:07:46,862
On September first, a Soviet fighter shot

207
00:07:46,882 --> 00:07:49,517
down Korean Air Lines Flight 007.

208
00:07:49,987 --> 00:07:52,690
All 269 people aboard died.

209
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:54,491
Reagan addressed the nation.

210
00:07:55,062 --> 00:07:57,878
The CIA's own historian later wrote that

211
00:07:57,898 --> 00:08:00,573
the shootdown raised Soviet fears of a

212
00:08:00,593 --> 00:08:02,346
possible American attack,

213
00:08:02,928 --> 00:08:05,482
and quote, made Moscow more suspicious

214
00:08:05,502 --> 00:08:08,191
that US military exercises might conceal

215
00:08:08,211 --> 00:08:10,495
preparations for an actual attack.

216
00:08:11,077 --> 00:08:12,977
Within weeks, he wrote, Soviet

217
00:08:12,997 --> 00:08:15,539
intelligence would react in exactly that

218
00:08:15,559 --> 00:08:15,795
way.

219
00:08:16,365 --> 00:08:18,549
The Advisory Board adds that Soviet

220
00:08:18,569 --> 00:08:20,941
officials, according to press reports,

221
00:08:20,961 --> 00:08:23,397
concluded the Reagan administration had

222
00:08:23,417 --> 00:08:25,853
deliberately engineered the incident to

223
00:08:25,873 --> 00:08:27,238
poison the atmosphere.

224
00:08:27,708 --> 00:08:29,569
The Board reports that as the Soviet

225
00:08:29,589 --> 00:08:30,144
conclusion.

226
00:08:30,484 --> 00:08:31,858
It is not a finding of fact.

227
00:08:32,428 --> 00:08:33,296
Then October.

228
00:08:33,766 --> 00:08:35,782
On the twenty-third, the bombing of the

229
00:08:35,802 --> 00:08:37,140
Marine barracks in Beirut.

230
00:08:37,610 --> 00:08:40,014
On the twenty-fifth, American troops land

231
00:08:40,034 --> 00:08:40,664
on Grenada.

232
00:08:41,134 --> 00:08:43,317
The Board says the Kremlin called the

233
00:08:43,337 --> 00:08:45,579
Grenada landing a bandit attack, and a

234
00:08:45,599 --> 00:08:47,543
crime against peace and humanity.

235
00:08:48,113 --> 00:08:50,375
And inside the Soviet Union, the Board

236
00:08:50,395 --> 00:08:52,596
says, a Western visitor reported that

237
00:08:52,616 --> 00:08:54,457
Andropov sent a letter to party

238
00:08:54,477 --> 00:08:56,378
organizations declaring that the

239
00:08:56,398 --> 00:08:58,239
motherland was truly in danger,

240
00:08:58,709 --> 00:09:00,919
and that there was no chance of improving

241
00:09:00,939 --> 00:09:02,713
relations with the United States.

242
00:09:03,183 --> 00:09:05,336
It was reportedly read at closed party

243
00:09:05,356 --> 00:09:06,936
meetings across the country.

244
00:09:07,506 --> 00:09:08,512
November seventh.

245
00:09:09,133 --> 00:09:10,342
Able Archer begins.

246
00:09:10,812 --> 00:09:13,533
The same day, according to press accounts

247
00:09:13,553 --> 00:09:16,074
cited in the Board's report, Politburo

248
00:09:16,094 --> 00:09:18,548
member Grigory Romanov told a Kremlin

249
00:09:18,568 --> 00:09:21,289
audience the international situation was,

250
00:09:21,309 --> 00:09:22,426
quote, white hot.

251
00:09:22,896 --> 00:09:24,031
Thoroughly white hot.

252
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:27,620
On November eighth or ninth, Moscow sent a

253
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:30,153
circular telegram to KGB offices in

254
00:09:30,173 --> 00:09:31,238
Western Europe.

255
00:09:31,708 --> 00:09:34,249
It ordered them to report on the increased

256
00:09:34,269 --> 00:09:36,139
alert status of American bases,

257
00:09:36,609 --> 00:09:38,364
and to check for indications of an

258
00:09:38,384 --> 00:09:39,668
impending nuclear attack.

259
00:09:40,138 --> 00:09:42,415
The London office read it as a sign of

260
00:09:42,435 --> 00:09:44,046
headquarters' RYAN concern.

261
00:09:44,616 --> 00:09:47,264
The best-known account comes from a KGB

262
00:09:47,284 --> 00:09:49,932
officer in London, Oleg Gordievsky, who

263
00:09:49,952 --> 00:09:52,122
was secretly working for British

264
00:09:52,142 --> 00:09:54,790
intelligence, as later described in the

265
00:09:54,810 --> 00:09:55,884
official record.

266
00:09:56,354 --> 00:09:59,392
A CIA historian summarizes it: the cable

267
00:09:59,412 --> 00:10:02,450
reportedly suggested American forces had

268
00:10:02,470 --> 00:10:03,520
gone on alert,

269
00:10:03,990 --> 00:10:06,029
and that the United States might have

270
00:10:06,049 --> 00:10:08,310
begun the countdown to a surprise nuclear

271
00:10:08,330 --> 00:10:08,532
war.

272
00:10:09,103 --> 00:10:10,387
What did the Soviets do?

273
00:10:10,857 --> 00:10:13,108
The Advisory Board lists reactions it

274
00:10:13,128 --> 00:10:14,828
calls unparalleled in scale.

275
00:10:15,248 --> 00:10:17,502
More than 36 intelligence flights,

276
00:10:17,522 --> 00:10:20,043
including missions over the Norwegian,

277
00:10:20,063 --> 00:10:22,116
North, Baltic and Barents Seas.

278
00:10:22,586 --> 00:10:24,921
A suspension of all flight operations

279
00:10:24,941 --> 00:10:27,213
except intelligence collection, from

280
00:10:27,233 --> 00:10:29,886
November fourth to tenth, probably to have

281
00:10:29,906 --> 00:10:32,559
as many aircraft as possible available for

282
00:10:32,579 --> 00:10:33,005
combat.

283
00:10:33,475 --> 00:10:36,061
And, the report says, transporting nuclear

284
00:10:36,081 --> 00:10:38,419
weapons from storage sites to delivery

285
00:10:38,439 --> 00:10:39,660
units by helicopter.

286
00:10:40,230 --> 00:10:42,170
Parts of that page remain blacked out.

287
00:10:42,752 --> 00:10:44,351
But the Board's reading is plain.

288
00:10:44,933 --> 00:10:47,703
The scale and timing, it wrote, strongly

289
00:10:47,723 --> 00:10:50,563
suggests that Soviet military leaders may

290
00:10:50,583 --> 00:10:53,423
have been seriously concerned that the US

291
00:10:53,443 --> 00:10:56,143
would use Able Archer 83 as a cover for

292
00:10:56,163 --> 00:10:57,817
launching a real attack.

293
00:10:58,387 --> 00:11:00,710
Most of this was not visible, in its

294
00:11:00,730 --> 00:11:03,313
totality, until long after the exercise.

295
00:11:03,783 --> 00:11:06,067
The Board says the full extent of the

296
00:11:06,087 --> 00:11:08,683
Soviet alert was not known until two weeks

297
00:11:08,703 --> 00:11:10,240
after the exercise ended,

298
00:11:10,710 --> 00:11:13,295
and the standdown had been in effect for

299
00:11:13,315 --> 00:11:15,835
nearly a week before fully armed MiG-23

300
00:11:15,855 --> 00:11:18,440
fighters were noted on air defense alert

301
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:19,482
in East Germany.

302
00:11:20,052 --> 00:11:21,969
According to the National Security

303
00:11:21,989 --> 00:11:23,678
Archive's summary, the Defense

304
00:11:23,698 --> 00:11:25,843
Intelligence Agency did not report the

305
00:11:25,863 --> 00:11:28,008
standdown until a week after it began,

306
00:11:28,478 --> 00:11:30,893
and no President's Daily Brief mentioned

307
00:11:30,913 --> 00:11:33,329
Able Archer or the Soviet response while

308
00:11:33,349 --> 00:11:34,364
it was happening.

309
00:11:34,834 --> 00:11:37,152
The President's own national security

310
00:11:37,172 --> 00:11:39,743
adviser, Robert McFarlane, told the Board

311
00:11:39,763 --> 00:11:42,208
he remembered hearing nothing about it.

312
00:11:42,778 --> 00:11:43,843
So who was watching?

313
00:11:44,463 --> 00:11:47,140
At U.S. Air Forces in Europe, the chief of

314
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:49,708
intelligence, Lieutenant General Leonard

315
00:11:49,728 --> 00:11:52,277
Perroots, saw Soviet aircraft going to a

316
00:11:52,297 --> 00:11:53,368
heightened alert.

317
00:11:53,838 --> 00:11:56,132
He had a choice: raise American readiness

318
00:11:56,152 --> 00:11:57,655
in response, or do nothing.

319
00:11:58,225 --> 00:11:59,989
He recommended not to respond.

320
00:12:00,571 --> 00:12:03,210
The Advisory Board calls that decision, in

321
00:12:03,230 --> 00:12:05,679
its words, fortuitous, if ill-informed.

322
00:12:06,261 --> 00:12:08,839
The officers in charge, it says, acted

323
00:12:08,859 --> 00:12:11,506
correctly out of instinct, not informed

324
00:12:11,526 --> 00:12:12,121
guidance.

325
00:12:12,691 --> 00:12:15,488
In January 1989, before retiring, Perroots

326
00:12:15,508 --> 00:12:18,170
sent a parting letter to the Director of

327
00:12:18,190 --> 00:12:20,785
Central Intelligence and to this Board,

328
00:12:21,255 --> 00:12:23,618
voicing his disquiet over the inadequate

329
00:12:23,638 --> 00:12:25,643
treatment of the Soviet war scare.

330
00:12:26,113 --> 00:12:28,325
The National Security Archive says the

331
00:12:28,345 --> 00:12:30,792
Board indicated it likely would never have

332
00:12:30,812 --> 00:12:32,671
studied the episode without him.

333
00:12:33,242 --> 00:12:34,776
The British saw the danger too.

334
00:12:35,246 --> 00:12:37,150
According to the Board, Britain's

335
00:12:37,170 --> 00:12:39,307
ambassador visited Under Secretary of

336
00:12:39,327 --> 00:12:40,882
State Lawrence Eagleburger.

337
00:12:41,352 --> 00:12:43,509
But the State Department's intelligence

338
00:12:43,529 --> 00:12:45,686
bureau presented a skeptical version of

339
00:12:45,706 --> 00:12:47,807
events, designed, in its officer's own

340
00:12:47,827 --> 00:12:49,649
words, to discourage the British.

341
00:12:50,219 --> 00:12:52,682
The National Security Archive adds that

342
00:12:52,702 --> 00:12:55,038
when the State Department requested a

343
00:12:55,058 --> 00:12:57,713
sanitized version of the estimate for NATO

344
00:12:57,733 --> 00:12:58,159
allies,

345
00:12:58,629 --> 00:13:01,240
it removed all mentions of Able Archer and

346
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:02,494
the Soviet response.

347
00:13:03,063 --> 00:13:03,968
And the President?

348
00:13:04,588 --> 00:13:07,044
A week after the exercise, on November

349
00:13:07,064 --> 00:13:09,520
eighteenth, Reagan wrote in his diary:

350
00:13:10,102 --> 00:13:12,623
I feel the Soviets are so defense minded,

351
00:13:12,643 --> 00:13:14,977
so paranoid about being attacked, that

352
00:13:14,997 --> 00:13:17,456
without being in any way soft on them we

353
00:13:17,476 --> 00:13:19,872
ought to tell them that no one here has

354
00:13:19,892 --> 00:13:22,474
any intention of doing anything like that.

355
00:13:23,044 --> 00:13:26,024
Seven months later, in June 1984, CIA

356
00:13:26,044 --> 00:13:29,348
Director William Casey sent the President

357
00:13:29,368 --> 00:13:32,429
and his top advisers a memo titled, US

358
00:13:32,449 --> 00:13:33,645
Soviet Tension.

359
00:13:34,115 --> 00:13:36,538
I attach here a rather stunning array of

360
00:13:36,558 --> 00:13:39,103
indicators of an increasing aggressiveness

361
00:13:39,123 --> 00:13:41,057
in Soviet policy and activities.

362
00:13:41,627 --> 00:13:43,758
The list included a ban on military help

363
00:13:43,778 --> 00:13:44,671
with the harvest,

364
00:13:45,141 --> 00:13:47,647
civilian aircraft production cut in favor

365
00:13:47,667 --> 00:13:49,064
of military transports,

366
00:13:49,534 --> 00:13:51,789
restrictions on Western diplomats' travel,

367
00:13:52,259 --> 00:13:54,997
and a Soviet media message likening U.S.

368
00:13:55,017 --> 00:13:57,616
Soviet relations to those between Nazi

369
00:13:57,636 --> 00:14:00,442
Germany and the Soviet Union before World

370
00:14:00,462 --> 00:14:00,924
War II.

371
00:14:01,494 --> 00:14:04,208
According to McFarlane, the Board reports,

372
00:14:04,228 --> 00:14:06,616
the President expressed surprise upon

373
00:14:06,636 --> 00:14:09,349
reading the memo, and described the events

374
00:14:09,369 --> 00:14:10,911
as, quote, really scary.

375
00:14:11,493 --> 00:14:13,742
McFarlane himself was less convinced.

376
00:14:14,325 --> 00:14:16,604
He wondered whether Moscow was trying to

377
00:14:16,624 --> 00:14:18,902
drive a wedge through Europe, to counter

378
00:14:18,922 --> 00:14:21,086
the President's missile defense plans.

379
00:14:21,656 --> 00:14:24,356
Journalist Don Oberdorfer, quoted in the

380
00:14:24,376 --> 00:14:26,940
CIA study, reported that Reagan asked,

381
00:14:27,410 --> 00:14:29,296
do you suppose they really believe that?

382
00:14:29,766 --> 00:14:32,383
The National Security Archive says he also

383
00:14:32,403 --> 00:14:34,769
asked his ambassador in Moscow, Arthur

384
00:14:34,789 --> 00:14:37,029
Hartman: do you think Soviet leaders

385
00:14:37,049 --> 00:14:39,603
really fear us, or is all the huffing and

386
00:14:39,623 --> 00:14:41,988
puffing just part of their propaganda?

387
00:14:42,558 --> 00:14:44,689
Whether the scare changed his thinking is

388
00:14:44,709 --> 00:14:45,948
a matter for historians.

389
00:14:46,418 --> 00:14:48,932
The CIA's own study notes that Britain's

390
00:14:48,952 --> 00:14:51,339
prime minister, Margaret Thatcher, was

391
00:14:51,359 --> 00:14:53,745
pressing the President to moderate his

392
00:14:53,765 --> 00:14:54,315
approach,

393
00:14:54,785 --> 00:14:56,887
and says some analysts suspected the

394
00:14:56,907 --> 00:14:59,245
British put their own spin on the Soviet

395
00:14:59,265 --> 00:14:59,834
reporting.

396
00:15:00,405 --> 00:15:01,310
So was it real?

397
00:15:01,930 --> 00:15:05,087
American intelligence's first answer, in

398
00:15:05,107 --> 00:15:06,120
1984, was no.

399
00:15:06,590 --> 00:15:08,625
A Special National Intelligence Estimate

400
00:15:08,645 --> 00:15:08,882
said:

401
00:15:09,352 --> 00:15:11,960
we believe strongly that Soviet actions

402
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:14,790
are not inspired by, and Soviet leaders do

403
00:15:14,810 --> 00:15:17,620
not perceive, a genuine danger of imminent

404
00:15:17,640 --> 00:15:20,382
conflict or confrontation with the United

405
00:15:20,402 --> 00:15:20,854
States.

406
00:15:21,424 --> 00:15:23,338
It judged the Soviet war talk to be

407
00:15:23,358 --> 00:15:23,945
propaganda,

408
00:15:24,415 --> 00:15:26,691
aimed at discrediting U.S. policies and

409
00:15:26,711 --> 00:15:28,280
mobilizing peace pressures.

410
00:15:28,750 --> 00:15:31,958
A CIA memo from December 1983 put it in

411
00:15:31,978 --> 00:15:33,116
similar terms:

412
00:15:33,586 --> 00:15:35,900
encourage resistance to the new missile

413
00:15:35,920 --> 00:15:38,294
deployments, deepen cleavages within the

414
00:15:38,314 --> 00:15:39,371
Atlantic alliance,

415
00:15:39,841 --> 00:15:42,248
and build public pressure on Washington

416
00:15:42,268 --> 00:15:44,426
toward a more conciliatory posture.

417
00:15:44,996 --> 00:15:46,696
And there is evidence for the skeptics.

418
00:15:47,166 --> 00:15:49,626
The CIA historian wrote that Able Archer

419
00:15:49,646 --> 00:15:52,106
seems to have made more of an impression

420
00:15:52,126 --> 00:15:54,524
on the White House than on the Kremlin.

421
00:15:54,994 --> 00:15:57,039
A senior Soviet affairs expert who

422
00:15:57,059 --> 00:15:59,408
questioned Soviet leaders reported that

423
00:15:59,428 --> 00:16:00,683
none had heard of it.

424
00:16:01,153 --> 00:16:03,813
A GRU officer said watch officers were

425
00:16:03,833 --> 00:16:06,634
concerned, but saw nothing else unusual.

426
00:16:07,204 --> 00:16:08,845
The Advisory Board disagreed.

427
00:16:09,428 --> 00:16:11,814
It said the evidence then did not, and

428
00:16:11,834 --> 00:16:14,092
certainly does not now, support such

429
00:16:14,112 --> 00:16:15,484
categoric conclusions.

430
00:16:16,067 --> 00:16:18,432
A strongly stated interpretation, it

431
00:16:18,452 --> 00:16:20,949
wrote, was defended by explaining away

432
00:16:20,969 --> 00:16:22,737
facts inconsistent with it.

433
00:16:23,308 --> 00:16:24,736
But the Board is careful too.

434
00:16:25,206 --> 00:16:27,226
It calls its reading a plausible

435
00:16:27,246 --> 00:16:29,584
interpretation based on a sizable but

436
00:16:29,604 --> 00:16:31,369
incomplete body of evidence.

437
00:16:31,839 --> 00:16:34,395
It says Soviet reactions were mixed, and

438
00:16:34,415 --> 00:16:37,035
that intelligence detected no large-scale

439
00:16:37,055 --> 00:16:38,323
Warsaw Pact buildup.

440
00:16:38,793 --> 00:16:40,858
And it writes that it does not intend this

441
00:16:40,878 --> 00:16:41,901
to be the final word.

442
00:16:42,470 --> 00:16:44,580
So the documents support two readings.

443
00:16:45,163 --> 00:16:47,281
One: a propaganda campaign that

444
00:16:47,301 --> 00:16:49,763
reverberated in Soviet bureaucracies

445
00:16:49,783 --> 00:16:52,521
without reflecting real fear at the top.

446
00:16:53,104 --> 00:16:55,243
The other: real fear, in part of the

447
00:16:55,263 --> 00:16:57,521
Soviet system, that a drill could be a

448
00:16:57,541 --> 00:16:58,659
cover for a strike.

449
00:16:59,242 --> 00:17:00,680
The Board favors the second.

450
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:04,092
The CIA historian agreed the KGB's alarm

451
00:17:04,112 --> 00:17:07,253
was real, but found Able Archer registered

452
00:17:07,273 --> 00:17:09,888
more in the White House than in the

453
00:17:09,908 --> 00:17:10,490
Kremlin.

454
00:17:11,061 --> 00:17:11,923
How did it end?

455
00:17:12,543 --> 00:17:15,110
By November eleventh the Soviet alert was

456
00:17:15,130 --> 00:17:16,371
evidently withdrawn.

457
00:17:16,841 --> 00:17:19,466
That day, Defense Minister Ustinov told

458
00:17:19,486 --> 00:17:22,111
senior officers that no enemy intrigues

459
00:17:22,131 --> 00:17:23,671
will catch us unawares.

460
00:17:24,141 --> 00:17:27,031
By autumn 1984, the Board says, the feared

461
00:17:27,051 --> 00:17:28,971
attack had not materialized,

462
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:32,334
and by early 1985 Soviet fear of one

463
00:17:32,354 --> 00:17:33,952
seemed to evaporate.

464
00:17:34,522 --> 00:17:36,436
The expected American attack never

465
00:17:36,456 --> 00:17:37,176
materialized.

466
00:17:37,646 --> 00:17:39,760
According to the Board, Ogarkov was

467
00:17:39,780 --> 00:17:41,101
removed from his post.

468
00:17:41,571 --> 00:17:43,612
Ustinov died on December twentieth.

469
00:17:44,082 --> 00:17:47,050
And when Chernenko died in March 1985,

470
00:17:47,070 --> 00:17:49,723
Gorbachev succeeded him as General

471
00:17:49,743 --> 00:17:51,531
Secretary the next day.

472
00:17:52,101 --> 00:17:54,373
But the intelligence community's last word

473
00:17:54,393 --> 00:17:54,974
was buried.

474
00:17:55,444 --> 00:17:58,271
A 1988 annex said Soviet concerns about

475
00:17:58,291 --> 00:18:00,680
the drift in superpower relations

476
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:03,527
indicated an increased threat of war in

477
00:18:03,547 --> 00:18:04,914
some leaders' eyes,

478
00:18:05,384 --> 00:18:07,834
and it sat in an annex to a tightly held

479
00:18:07,854 --> 00:18:10,119
estimate, not meant for policymakers.

480
00:18:10,688 --> 00:18:12,980
The Board's executive summary carries a

481
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:14,462
warning about the future.

482
00:18:14,883 --> 00:18:17,599
It is an especially grave error, it wrote,

483
00:18:17,619 --> 00:18:20,336
to assume that since we know the US is not

484
00:18:20,356 --> 00:18:22,812
going to start World War III, the next

485
00:18:22,832 --> 00:18:25,418
leaders of the Kremlin will also believe

486
00:18:25,438 --> 00:18:27,308
that, and act on that belief.

487
00:18:27,878 --> 00:18:31,071
Nobody started a war in November 1983.

488
00:18:31,541 --> 00:18:33,872
A routine drill, which nothing in these

489
00:18:33,892 --> 00:18:36,224
records suggests was meant as a threat,

490
00:18:36,244 --> 00:18:38,575
may have been read by some on the other

491
00:18:38,595 --> 00:18:40,505
side as the opening move of one.

492
00:18:40,975 --> 00:18:43,305
The documents do not tell us how close it

493
00:18:43,325 --> 00:18:43,592
came.

494
00:18:44,062 --> 00:18:46,170
They tell us who was worried, what they

495
00:18:46,190 --> 00:18:48,299
did, and who in Washington noticed, and

496
00:18:48,319 --> 00:18:48,572
when.

497
00:18:49,142 --> 00:18:50,495
Here is our question for you.

498
00:18:50,965 --> 00:18:53,007
Was Able Archer a near miss, or a scare

499
00:18:53,027 --> 00:18:54,329
that grew in the telling?

500
00:18:54,799 --> 00:18:57,459
The Advisory Board and the CIA historian

501
00:18:57,479 --> 00:19:00,207
differ on how much Able Archer registered

502
00:19:00,227 --> 00:19:01,213
in the Kremlin.

503
00:19:01,683 --> 00:19:03,906
Tell us in the comments which reading the

504
00:19:03,926 --> 00:19:06,094
documents support, and please keep it to

505
00:19:06,114 --> 00:19:06,860
the documents.

506
00:19:07,430 --> 00:19:09,732
Next file: another secret the government

507
00:19:09,752 --> 00:19:10,719
kept for decades.
